<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Ceh Pech</h1><p class="st-meta">1441 CE – 1541 CE (approx.) · capital Motul</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Maya province of north-western Yucatán ruled by the Pech lineage from Motul, an early ally of the Spanish conquerors.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ceh Pech emerged after the collapse of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/league-of-mayapan">League of Mayapan</a> around 1441, under the Pech lineage, which had been prominent at Mayapan. Its territory lay along the northern coast between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chakan">Chakan</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ah-kin-chel">Ah Kin Chel</a>, with its capital at Motul.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The province was ruled by lords of the Pech lineage over towns such as Conkal, Chicxulub and Motul. Coastal salt production and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> farming supported it. The Pech chose to ally with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-montejo-the-younger">Francisco de Montejo the Younger</a> in 1541, and one of its nobles, Nakuk Pech, later wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-chicxulub">Chronicle of Chicxulub</a>, a Maya account of the conquest.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In return for their alliance, Pech nobles kept privileges under Spanish rule, and the province was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-yucatan">Province of Yucatán</a>. Motul became a Franciscan centre.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ceh-pech.jpg" alt="Ceh Pech" width="320" height="233"><figcaption>Image: Jaontiveros · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cacicazgos_mayas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1441 CE – 1541 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Motul</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Maya religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Yucatec Maya</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pech province</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1491&amp;focus=ceh-pech" aria-label="Ceh Pech on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2280 1398 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ceh Pech lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2600 1654L2591 1670M2600 1654L4910 989M2600 1654L2590 1658"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2591 1670h0M4910 989h0M2590 1658h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2600 1654h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2600 1654h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Motul. <a href="/atlas?year=1491&amp;focus=ceh-pech">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
